WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.181 --> 00:00:08.570
SPEAKER 1: Bradley J Birzer
is the Russell Amos Kirk

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:12.200
professor in American
studies at Hillsdale College.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.090
He earned his BA
from the University

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:19.130
of Notre Dame and his MA and
PhD from Indiana University.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.950
He served on the Board of
the Free Enterprise Institute

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.470
and the Center for
Cultural Renewal.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.810
He also serves as
fellow or scholar

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:30.470
at the Foundation for Economic
Education, the Intercollegiate

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:34.220
Studies Institute, the
McConnell Center, and the Center

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.590
for Economic Personalism.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.920
He has written for
numerous publications,

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.270
including The
Imaginative Conservative,

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:45.980
a site he co-founded, Ignatius
Insight, National Review,

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.450
and Catholic World Report.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.180
He is the author of several
books, including J.R.R.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.850
Tolkien's Sanctifying Myth--

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.940
Understanding Middle
Earth, American Cicero--

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.320
The Life of Charles Carroll, and
In Defense of Andrew Jackson.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.670
Please welcome Dr. Birzer.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.163
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:12.107 --> 00:01:14.190
BRADLEY J BIRZER: Well,
good afternoon, everybody.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:18.200
I am very, very honored to be
here and to start off this CCA.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.400
I've never been able to--

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.380
I've never been asked
to speak at a CCA

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.380
as an actual participant
during the lectures.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.870
My department chair,
who's been very gracious

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.110
and has been Dean
of Faculty, has

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.385
asked me to speak on
faculty roundtables before,

00:01:33.385 --> 00:01:35.510
but this is the first time
I actually get to speak.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.730
And I get to speak as
the first speaker, which

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.310
is really, really exciting
in all kinds of ways.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.650
So, Jason, thank you so
much for that introduction.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.840
And I also would like to think
Matt Bell for inviting me,

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.280
