00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.050
he knows I recognized him.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:52.390
So we will leave it at that.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:52.990
OK.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:57.020
On a very serious note,
as many of you may know,

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.830
when Andrew Jackson
took the oath of office

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.240
for the very first time
on March 4 of 1829,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.130
he did so wearing all black.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:09.430
He was still lamenting
the very recent loss

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:14.260
of his wife, his partner, his
absolute intellectual equal.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:19.000
He also-- when he gave his talk,
when he gave his inaugural,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.090
he bowed twice to
the American people--

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.450
once when he started and once
when he finished his talks.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.240
And both times he
recognized that they were

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.090
the true sovereigns of America.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.050
This was not affectation
or theater on his part.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:37.510
One of the things that
astounded me absolutely

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.180
in getting to write
on Andrew Jackson

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.150
is how unbelievably
honest he was

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.660
and how unbelievably dedicated
he was to the American people.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.100
So in 2017-- and I don't want to
make this too autobiographical

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.150
about me.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.300
But in 2017, I got asked
through John Miller, who

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.950
teaches here at the college--

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:00.650
he was asked by Harry Crocker,
who was the head then,

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.530
of Regnery Publishing--

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.320
if they knew somebody who could
write a very quick and short

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.390
biography of Andrew Jackson.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.630
I'd been teaching Andrew
Jackson during a class

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:14.770
that we have here at Hillsdale
College for the last 20, 21

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.670
years, since 1999.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.930
I'd been teaching a course
called Jacksonian America.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:21.670
And throughout most
of the time that I

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.260
had the privilege of
teaching that course-- which

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:24.310
I absolutely loved.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.220
There are incredible figures
like Ralph Waldo Emerson

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.100
and Henry David Thoreau
and John Quincy Adams.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.840
During much of that,
I always taught

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.850
Andrew Jackson is the bad guy.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.670
He was the first really
strong executive.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.340
This was a person
who tried to distort

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.540
what the founders wanted.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:42.010
He was our seventh president.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:45.340
He was the first non-classically
educated president.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:47.320
He was kind of a
country bumpkin.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.940
He was an Indian murderer.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.490
And I taught him,
basically, as that

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:54.310
for roughly 18
years of my career

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:56.260
here at Hillsdale College.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:58.360
So when John Miller
and Harry Crocker

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.210
asked me to write
about Andrew Jackson,

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:02.325
I was kind of taken aback.

00:05:02.325 --> 00:05:03.700
I wasn't quite
sure if I could do

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.520
this, because I know
that they wanted a pretty

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.300
positive biography, especially
because Donald Trump had been

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.300
making all kinds of claims very
recently, especially on Andrew

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:16.900
Jackson's 250th birthday,
about the greatness

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:17.710
of that President.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.560
And I knew that Regnery
wanted to kind of compare

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.560
Andrew Jackson to Donald Trump.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.420
And I decided to
go ahead, because I

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.498
got from Harry Crocker
an assurance that I

00:05:27.498 --> 00:05:29.290
could write whatever
I wanted, as long as I

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.030
was able to back it up.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.480
I found very quickly that I fell
in love with Andrew Jackson,

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:35.470
almost immediately.

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:38.170
Of all the people I've had
the privilege to write on,

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:41.620
and I've written only upon
people that I really like,

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.530
I found that I got to
know Andrew Jackson better

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:46.960
than almost every
other figure that I've

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.310
been able to write about.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:52.540
And the reason was because
he speaks so openly.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:56.290
There is no duplicity in
his language, in his mind.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:58.870
I actually think it
would go constitutionally

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.080
against the very character
and the very soul

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.330
and person of Andrew
Jackson to be duplicitous.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.110
Now, he's also the
most brutal person

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:11.170
I have ever come
across in my own life,

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.300
in terms of studying
someone this intimately.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.058
So he was both incredibly
honest and incredibly brutal.

00:06:18.058 --> 00:06:19.600
And I had to come
to grips with that.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.370
What is it about
this man, and what

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:23.920
is it about his brutality,
that in many ways

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:25.120
plays into his honesty?

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:28.990
And what is it about his honesty
that plays into his brutality?

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.780
What I wasn't expecting,
and partly because I

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:36.010
was in grad school during the
1990s, what I wasn't expecting

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.650
was the vigorous
pushback that I was

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:43.240
going to get from academics
in even quasi-praising

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.850
any aspect of Andrew Jackson.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:47.000
When I was in college--

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:51.470
I graduated college in 1990 and
I finished grad school in 1999.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.230
When I was in college
and graduate school,

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:56.020
Andrew Jackson was
still very well regarded

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.700
within the history profession.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.450
In fact, a poll among
historians in 1996

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:04.690
revealed that most
American historians still

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.880
regarded Andrew Jackson
as one of the five

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.400
most important and best
presidents in the United

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.730
States.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:15.220
And that's amazing, because
if you go back now in 2020

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:17.110
and you were to ask
historians what they

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:20.230
think of Andrew Jackson,
he'll be probably

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:22.390
in the bottom 10 to 15.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:26.060
His star has fallen
dramatically.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.330
And I was told, in
no uncertain terms,

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:31.060
both in interviews on the
radio, as well as in critiques

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:34.900
of my own book, that defending
Andrew Jackson was no better

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:37.210
than defending Adolf Hitler.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:38.860
I do not exaggerate.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.280
I was told this several times.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:42.863
Of course, they never
bring up Stalin.

00:07:42.863 --> 00:07:44.363
And I thought, well,
if you're going

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:46.870
to get nasty, bring Stalin
up, but they didn't all right.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:50.680
He's too left, so only Hitler
would do in a criticism.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:54.730
But I was told that Andrew
Jackson was a rapist.

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:56.270
That's not true.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:58.390
I was told he was a murderer.

00:07:58.390 --> 00:07:59.900
That's not true.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:01.960
And I was told he was
an ethnic cleanser.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.460
That's not true.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:08.770
Fascinating what so many people
believe about Andrew Jackson.

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:12.730
And what struck, me even in
the pushback on Andrew Jackson,

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:17.110
was how absolutely
mythic this character is.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.240
He is either in
American history,

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:24.940
and has been, either a kind of
heroic demigod, the great flag

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:28.030
embodied, or he
has been a demon.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.690
In his own days, he
was called a knight

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:34.419
in buckskin, the Napoleon
of the woods, Old Hickory.

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:36.940
And the greatest title
that any American

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:39.679
could have held prior
to the 20th century,

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:41.890
he was the hero of New Orleans.

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:44.770
Those were all very
nice accolades.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:48.470
These were great things that
we saw about his character.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.140
And I can't help but
think that there's

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:54.340
a kind of American
stereotype, in the good way,

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:57.040
about who Andrew Jackson was.

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.450
He is like this kind of
unrealistic, but fascinating

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:02.960
American character.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:06.220
He is really the
frontier personified.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:10.600
I couldn't help but think of
Johnny Ringo in Stagecoach,

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.990
that great John Ford movie.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:16.738
And I couldn't help but think of
the innocence of Johnny Ringo.

00:09:16.738 --> 00:09:18.280
For those of you've
seen Stagecoach--

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:20.230
and if you haven't
recently, you should,

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:23.350
one of the great classics
in American cinema.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:25.390
Johnny Ringo, this
young gunfighter,

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:28.720
finds himself on a stage
coach with a whole variety

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:32.120
of different people from
different parts of society.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.620
And one of the persons on the
stage coach is a prostitute.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:37.420
And Johnny Ringo
doesn't get that.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:41.380
He just knows that she's a
fancy woman and she's a woman,

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:43.870
and he needs to treat
her in a certain way.

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:45.610
The other woman
on the stage coach

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:47.630
is deeply offended by this.

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:50.440
I'll come back to why this
is important, especially

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:51.910
for Andrew Jackson.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:56.080
Or I think of John Wayne,
again, as Ethan Edwards

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.530
in The Searchers,
another John Ford movie.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.800
John Ford and John Wayne
understood something

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:04.780
about this unique character--

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:09.280
the man who loses, the man who
struggles no matter the cost.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:12.100
But no matter the loss and
no matter the struggle, he

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.350
continues to fight
for what's right,

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:17.560
because he knows that's
at the essence of what it

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:20.710
means to be an American male.

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:22.850
But we could jump forward
a little bit, as well.

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:25.660
And now I'm probably revealing
my own age, because I certainly

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:30.380
wasn't around when Stagecoach
or The Searchers came out,

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.880
but I was very much alive and
rather in love with a science

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:38.110
fiction story called
Firefly in the early 2000s,

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:39.557
about 20 years ago.

00:10:39.557 --> 00:10:41.140
And for those of you
who've seen that,

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:43.600
it is a Western set in space.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.060
And the main figure
is a John Wayne kind

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:48.460
of figure by the name
of Malcolm Reynolds.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.100
And there's a great moment
in the pilot of that show.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:53.440
The show only lasted,
I think, 16 episodes.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:56.800
They did 13 for the pilot
season and then another three

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:57.620
and it failed.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.110
And then they made a movie.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:01.720
It's unfortunate that it failed,
but it's one of those things,

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:03.560
I'm glad it existed at all.

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.950
But there's a moment where
a young doctor comes up

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:08.240
to the captain of the
ship, the Firefly--

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.320
or the Serenity, a
Firefly-class ship.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:14.320
And this character, Malcolm
Reynolds, is challenged.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.770
And the doctor says, after
all the violence I've seen,

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:19.370
are you going to shoot me?

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:21.790
And Malcolm Reynolds
says something to him

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:23.320
that's incredible.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.630
And Andrew Jackson
could have said it.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:28.360
He says, "You don't
know me, son, so

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:31.000
let me explain it to you once.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.390
If I ever kill you, you'll be
awake, you'll be facing me,

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:38.030
and you'll be armed."

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:42.530
Now, that's the ultimate
American male statement.

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:44.480
In terms of
understanding what it

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:47.750
means to be a small-r
republican, a believer

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:50.750
in a republic, and
to be an American,

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:53.330
I think that captures
the essence of it.

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:56.720
Now, it's not a normal
statement and yet, it's

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.150
unbelievably American.

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:03.840
And for me, this is what
Andrew Jackson was all about.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.090
So I want to make
five points today

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:10.940
in talking about character and
leadership in Andrew Jackson.

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:17.840
So Andrew Jackson is born in
1767, and he dies in 1845.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:20.625
It's a pretty amazing life,
when you think about everything

00:12:20.625 --> 00:12:21.500
that he went through.

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:25.300
He was born into absolute
poverty, Scotch-Irish poverty,

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:26.390
in every way.

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:29.280
He was able to work his
way up into the world,

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.030
even though he had lost
the entirety of his family

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:34.250
by the time he was 14.

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:38.450
He was able to work his way
up out of poverty and into not

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:41.570
just respectable middle
class, but Southern gentry

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:45.470
status in Nashville,
Tennessee, which I would argue

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:48.080
was as Western
and frontier as it

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:51.320
was southern during the
age of Andrew Jackson--

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:54.230
again, those two
qualities coming together,

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:56.780
that violence of the
frontier and that

00:12:56.780 --> 00:13:00.380
honor of being a
Scotch-Irish American.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:03.020
But I want to point out five
things about Andrew Jackson,

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:05.670
as we think about
him and his career.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.430
Number one, I think
very importantly,

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:12.020
he was, all of his life,
a small-r Republican.

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.540
He believed in the
essence of a republic,

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:17.840
he was a Presbyterian,
and he was Scotch-Irish.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:21.830
And I think in many ways that
identification of his ethnicity

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.620
and his religion
and his politics

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:27.800
reveals much about
who the man was.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:30.150
His mother, as I mentioned,
died when he was young.

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:31.880
He was only 14.

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:35.450
And his mother died because she
was nursing American soldiers

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:38.120
during the American
Revolution and caught a fever

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.440
and was not able to overcome it.

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.650
But she did leave some
advice for Andrew,

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:45.860
whom she hoped would grow up
to be a Presbyterian minister.

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:49.400
It was actually one reason that
they saved up their money, very

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:51.470
little that they
had, for Andrew to be

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:53.090
able to go to a
liberal arts college

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:55.160
when he was a young man.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:56.870
He didn't use the
money for that.

00:13:56.870 --> 00:13:58.340
It went for other things.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:00.500
Andrew Jackson did
end up having somewhat

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:03.650
of a classical education,
but as he himself said,

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.260
everything that he
ever needed to know,

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:10.250
he learned by reading
stories of William Wallace,

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:12.710
the great Scottish nationalist.

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:14.870
And there's something to
that, especially when we

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:16.700
think about Andrew Jackson.

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:19.100
But here's what his
mother wrote him,

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:22.130
before she died, as
a piece of advice.

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:25.190
So point number one
being Republican,

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:30.260
Scotch-Irish, Presbyterian, and
being the son of this woman.

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:32.660
"Andrew, if I should
never see you again,

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.180
I wish you to remember and
treasure up some things that I

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.770
have already said to you.

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:40.700
In this world, you always
have to make your own way.

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:43.200
To do that, you have
to have friends.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.410
You must make friends
by being honest.

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:48.260
And you must keep them
by being steadfast.

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:49.880
You must keep in
mind that friends

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:52.700
worth having will
in the long run

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:56.280
expect as much from you
as they give to you.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:00.410
"To forget an obligation or to
be ungrateful for a kindness

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:04.610
is a base crime, not
merely a fault or a sin,

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:07.280
but an actual crime.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:11.480
Men guilty of this crime sooner
or later suffer the penalty.

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:16.250
In personal conduct, be always
polite, but never obsequious.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:20.570
None will respect you more than
you will ever respect yourself.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:23.750
Avoid quarrels as
long as you can

00:15:23.750 --> 00:15:29.000
without yielding to imposition,
but sustain your manhood

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.290
always.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:33.830
Never bring a suit of law
for assault or battery

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:36.650
or defamation to a courtroom.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:40.010
The law affords no
remedy for such outrages

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:43.460
that can satisfy the
feelings of a true man."

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:46.070
Everybody catch that?

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:49.130
You beat the snot out of
your opponent on the street.

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:51.980
You don't take him to court.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:55.520
This is what it means to
be a real man of the law.

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:59.090
"Never wound the
feelings of another.

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:03.380
Never brook once an outrage
upon your own feelings.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:05.990
If you ever have to
vindicate your feelings

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:09.050
or defend your
honor, do it calmly.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:12.350
And then, if angry,
wait until your wrath

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:15.540
cools before you proceed."

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:18.060
Now, what motherly
advice, right?

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:19.050
There we have it.

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:21.180
And Andrew loves this.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.010
This is everything to him.

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:24.900
And one of the most
fascinating things

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:28.440
I found in going through
the life of Andrew Jackson

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:32.160
was how much, to an almost
surrealistic degree,

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:33.900
he respected women.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:35.580
Now, this is a
great thing, right?

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:37.320
We should respect women.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.210
Men should respect women.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:40.980
But Jackson took
this to the point

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.410
where his mother
and his wife, who

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:47.430
was his intellectual equal
in every way and his partner,

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:52.170
he took it so far that I have
never seen anyone in history

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:55.020
think so highly of a
woman, except for maybe

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:58.260
Roman Catholics in the way
they approach the Virgin Mary.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:01.620
And I say that without
any kind of recrimination,

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:03.150
just straightforward.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:05.907
Jackson had a great
reverence for women.

00:17:05.907 --> 00:17:07.990
So if you remember, I
brought up a little bit ago,

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:10.470
Johnny Ringo on the stagecoach.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:14.790
We find letters in Andrew
Jackson's collected letters

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:17.470
where he wrote to the
prostitutes of Nashville,

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.460
Tennessee inviting
them to the town balls,

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:23.550
not as dates, but as
equals, because they, too,

00:17:23.550 --> 00:17:27.960
were citizens or at least
residents of Nashville.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.190
Wasn't a joke.

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:32.640
It wasn't meant in any
way to be derogatory.

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:36.703
Andrew truly believed that
this was how you treated women.

00:17:36.703 --> 00:17:38.370
And we could say, and
this was something

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:39.787
that was brought
up against Andrew

00:17:39.787 --> 00:17:42.570
Jackson in his own
lifetime and today,

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:43.980
well, what about Black women?

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:45.820
He was a slave owner.

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:48.240
We have the testimony
of one of his slaves,

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.780
Hannah, who said that
every time Andrew

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:55.680
Jackson came to the plantation,
everybody celebrated.

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:56.820
He solved problems.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:58.210
He served as a judge.

00:17:58.210 --> 00:18:01.260
He treated all of his
slaves with respect.

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:04.200
He rolled up his shirt
sleeves and worked with them

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:05.250
in every way.

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:08.550
And not only that, but when
Rachel Jackson passed away

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:12.180
in 1829 and Jackson
had to leave his estate

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.550
to go up to Washington
to be president,

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:17.430
he left Hannah, his
Black slave, in charge

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:22.090
of the estate for the eight
years that he was gone.

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:23.350
It's pretty impressive.

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:26.430
Kind of complicates our
view of slavery, doesn't it?

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:27.030
There it is.

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:29.370
Very, very interesting.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:32.400
Point number two
about Andrew Jackson.

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:35.730
As I mentioned earlier,
Jackson was as honest

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:37.290
as he was violent.

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:39.940
And one of the things that
shocked me to no end--

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:41.850
and I will admit,
I'm from Kansas.

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:44.460
I'm a Volga German,
Russian-German stock.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:48.330
My family refused to fight
during World War I, which

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:50.460
my wife's family still
likes to tease me about,

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:52.544
because they have
fought in every,

00:18:52.544 --> 00:18:55.320
every encounter America's been
in, I think going back to,

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:56.760
like, Queen Anne's war.

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:59.910
So they have been in
everything and very proudly so.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:04.350
My family has done everything
to dodge war in every way.

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:07.660
So this is not from my
background in Kansas.

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:11.010
But I think about the
complex set of rules

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:15.900
that went around
dueling during the 1800s

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:19.080
and how complicated this was.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:23.070
Andrew Jackson once said,
for malicious slander,

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:27.510
all men are answered
only at the bar of honor.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:29.710
Now, think about what
his mother told him.

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:32.160
Anytime you're wronged,
you never take it to court.

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:33.940
You settle it in the street.

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:35.720
That's what real men do.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:38.160
And that's exactly what
Andrew Jackson did.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.470
But there's something
about Jackson's duels

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:43.060
that are absolutely fascinating.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:49.350
The duels dictated that no
people ever of unequal worth

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:51.480
could challenge one
another in a duel.

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:54.450
And Jackson has in his own
letters and his diaries that

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:56.880
when younger men who were
clearly trying to make

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:00.270
their way in society and prove
their worth within Scotch-Irish

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:01.290
society--

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:03.330
when they would
challenge Jackson,

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:04.830
Jackson would
actually go up to them

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.810
in the street with his
cane and just beat them.

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:08.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:10.710
He would tell them, you're
not worthy of a duel,

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:12.210
but someday you will be.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:15.810
And it was actually-- to
be caned by Andrew Jackson

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:17.520
was regarded as a good thing.

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:19.350
And here's why.

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:23.010
Because it meant that you
would mean something someday,

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:24.690
even if you don't yet.

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:29.187
If Jackson ignored you, that
was the worst thing possible.

00:20:29.187 --> 00:20:31.020
Here's one of the most
interesting comments.

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:35.220
And I'll give you two stories
about Jackson and dueling.

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:36.690
When he was in the US Senate--

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:38.790
and he only served
twice in the US Senate

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:40.770
and neither for a full
term, just a couple

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:45.660
of years in the 1790s and
then again in the 1820s.

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:47.910
He just served for
about two years each.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:50.070
But the first time
that he was in,

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:52.780
he was in during John
Adams's administration.

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:56.670
And he was absolutely disgusted
by a fight that was going

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:59.940
on-- not a real fight, but
a political argument going

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:01.440
on the floor of the Senate--

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:04.260
that lasted for 16 days.

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:08.190
And Jackson writes in his
diary, "I don't get this.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:10.440
These men are a feat.

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:12.900
This is all sticks and spittle.

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:20.550
We have spent 16 days of the
people's money spending $10,000

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:21.910
for this debate.

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:24.280
And what an absolute disgrace.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.420
They could have easily
solved it by a quick duel

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:28.530
outside of Congress."

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:29.640
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.680
Now, he means this.

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:33.780
This is not a joke
on his part, right?

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:35.920
This is the way
men settle things.

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:37.680
And I have to admit,
and I don't consider

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:39.960
myself an extremely
violent person,

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:41.850
but when I looked
at how complicated

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:45.930
the rules were in society and
how detailed the instructions

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:48.900
were on who you could fight
and who you couldn't fight,

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:52.680
there is something honorable
in what Jackson is advocating.

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:54.780
I'm not calling for a return--

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:56.160
please, I can see people now.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:58.830
"Hillsdale professor calls
for a return to dueling."

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:00.640
This is not what I'm doing.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:03.450
But I do want to put it into
the context of the times,

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:06.060
that there is something
fascinating about what's

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:06.640
going on.

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:08.790
But here's my
favorite challenge.

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:12.510
Andrew Jackson, as you may
know, was extremely intelligent,

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:14.430
but not very well educated.

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:17.370
And one of the funniest things
about going through his letters

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:20.850
is he never spells the same
word the same way twice, right?

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:22.350
And I think he would
have considered

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:24.330
it a badge of
dishonor had he been

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:26.280
able to spell consistently.

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:28.950
But he would write these
challenges in the Nashville

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:33.090
newspaper where he would
say, because of this person's

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:36.840
actions, I challenge this person
to a duel for whatever reason.

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:37.890
And he would list it.

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:40.170
And then he would say,
I call on this person

00:22:40.170 --> 00:22:41.970
to give me a time and a date.

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:44.700
And one of the persons that
he got into a fight with

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.250
was the governor, John Sevier.

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:49.590
And so they were trying
to figure out a time.

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:51.840
And they kept sending-- and
I've seen all these notes.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:53.040
They're hilarious.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:55.620
They keep sending these notes
back to each other saying,

00:22:55.620 --> 00:22:58.200
well, I think we should
fight by long sword.

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:00.300
We'll do it at this
place at this time.

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:03.030
Other one will write back, no,
long sword's not appropriate.

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:05.280
I say we use this
caliber pistol.

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:06.330
We'll do it at this time.

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:08.790
And this went on for weeks.

00:23:08.790 --> 00:23:11.430
And Jackson got
really frustrated.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:14.610
And so he finally
published in the newspaper,

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:18.420
"To all whom shall see
this, present greetings.

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:21.710
Know ye that I, Andrew
Jackson, do pronounce, publish,

00:23:21.710 --> 00:23:25.220
and declare to the world
that the excellency John

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:28.430
Sevier, your governor,
captain general,

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:31.160
commander in chief of the land
and Naval forces of the state

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:33.215
of Tennessee is a base coward.

00:23:33.215 --> 00:23:35.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:38.210
"He will basely insult,
but he has not the courage

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:40.500
to repair the wound."

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:42.350
Now, there is the
final statement.

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:43.670
And so Sevier agrees.

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:45.350
And they come up on a time.

00:23:45.350 --> 00:23:48.140
And Jackson is anxious
about this duel.

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:51.470
And they have to duel right on
the Tennessee-Kentucky border.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.090
They don't want to
be either exactly

00:23:53.090 --> 00:23:55.220
in Kentucky or Tennessee
in case the law

00:23:55.220 --> 00:23:57.330
decides to get involved.

00:23:57.330 --> 00:24:00.530
So they pick this spot on the
Kentucky-Tennessee border.

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:02.510
Jackson shows up early.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:03.560
He's got his number two.

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:06.440
You always have a second
with you, in case you fall,

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:08.120
who can pick up the fight.

00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:10.610
And he sees, about
15 minutes late,

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:15.260
Sevier riding in on a horse
with a friend, his number two.

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:18.470
And Jackson is furious,
because the guy's late.

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:20.450
And so Jackson pulls his cane.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:21.890
Remember the cane?

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:26.840
He pulls his cane out,
and he charges Sevier,

00:24:26.840 --> 00:24:29.090
charges him across this field.

00:24:29.090 --> 00:24:32.630
And Sevier is so
befuddled by this

00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:35.180
that he tries to
pull his sword out,

00:24:35.180 --> 00:24:37.280
but in the act of trying
to pull his sword out,

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:39.110
he falls off his horse.

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:41.540
And he falls onto the
ground on top of his sword,

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:44.310
and his sword breaks in two.

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:46.140
Jackson comes up
to him and says,

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:48.630
I see you've removed your sword.

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:51.036
I consider this duel satisfied.

00:24:51.036 --> 00:24:52.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:56.340
They shook hands, rode
back to Nashville together,

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:58.710
and became constant
allies for the rest

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:00.060
of each of their career.

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:01.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:05.890
How do we explain this in
politically correct 2020,

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:06.580
right?

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:07.890
Can you imagine on Twitter?

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:12.270
[LAUGHS] Oh, you scoundrel.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:16.260
All right, point number
three, and specifically about

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:20.670
the context of this great CCA
on character and leadership

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:22.043
in the military.

00:25:22.043 --> 00:25:23.460
One of the most
interesting things

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:26.760
about Andrew Jackson that I
think we often don't remember

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:31.800
about him is that he actually
had a great animosity

00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:34.140
towards the standing
army, even though he

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:36.660
was a general within
the standing army.

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:38.820
He wrote constantly
throughout his life.

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:40.800
He didn't write-- he
wrote lots of letters.

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:45.090
He didn't write a lot of public
documents, but he did a few.

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:49.680
And in these statements that he
wrote about the standing army,

00:25:49.680 --> 00:25:54.420
he had kind of the old fashioned
small-r republican notion that

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:57.660
a true republic will be
defended by its Navy--

00:25:57.660 --> 00:26:01.560
it can be permanent-- but
never by a standing army, which

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:05.670
is almost always the tool of
oppression, as he thought.

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:08.010
He said, there are
really only two reasons

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:09.510
to call up the army.

00:26:09.510 --> 00:26:11.610
And you never have it
for more than two years.

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:13.290
And this is in the Constitution.

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:16.800
We know that no bill that
gives revenue to the army

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:18.610
can last for more
than two years.

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:21.000
There are no such
restrictions on the Navy,

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.850
but there is that in
the US constitution.

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:26.370
Jackson said there are only
two reasons we would ever

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:28.110
call up the standing army.

00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:31.230
Number one, if the
Indians are harassing us.

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:33.770
Or number two, if
the Europeans are.

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:36.540
He's not expecting anyone
else, but if the Europeans

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:37.890
or the Indians.

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:40.740
And even then, when we
call up to standing army,

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:42.900
we always put limitations on it.

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:45.720
And we always make sure
that the militia is

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:47.970
superior to the standing army.

00:26:47.970 --> 00:26:54.360
Here he is an 1805, during his
July 4th toast in Tennessee.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:57.270
He says, and this
is toast number--

00:26:57.270 --> 00:26:58.790
oh, I'm sorry, I
did not write this.

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:00.300
It's toast number seven.

00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:02.580
And it actually mattered,
the number of toast,

00:27:02.580 --> 00:27:05.520
because I'll read you
seven and then 11.

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:07.410
So toast number seven.

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:09.630
"To the militias of
the United States,

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:12.780
the surest bulwark of freedom."

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:14.880
And then number 11.

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:17.490
"To the Army of
the United States,

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:19.680
which we must
always remember can

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:22.350
be doomed to
persecution and tyranny

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:25.800
under a government of laws
to satiate the private spleen

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:29.180
of any would-be despot."

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:31.400
So that's pretty amazing.

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:33.620
We're praising the
army, but not really,

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:37.070
because we don't know exactly
what that army is going to do.

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:41.060
Five years later, in 1810,
he explained his own position

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:43.400
in a letter to the
governor of Tennessee.

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:46.040
He says, "Our independence
and liberty as a people

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:48.410
was not obtained
without expense.

00:27:48.410 --> 00:27:51.470
It was dearly bought
with blood and treasure.

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:53.370
And it must be preserved.

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:56.090
The pence on this subject
can never be counted,

00:27:56.090 --> 00:27:59.300
but the only real and
substantial defense

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:03.140
of a free people is
the organized militia.

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:06.530
This is always," and
I quote from Jackson,

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:10.370
"in contrast with the
corruption manifested

00:28:10.370 --> 00:28:13.220
at all times in
our regular army."

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:15.950
And this has been verified
by our constitution, which

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:20.570
only allows the two-year
funding for things in the army.

00:28:20.570 --> 00:28:25.010
In 1812 and 1813, when
Andrew Jackson called up

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:30.020
his own private militia and
state army within Tennessee,

00:28:30.020 --> 00:28:32.210
he told them that no matter
what-- he assured them,

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:34.310
he would not allow
the federal government

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:36.770
to federalize them in any way.

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:39.530
He said to his men
in March of 1812,

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:42.350
there will be no drafts
or compulsory levies.

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:45.350
And if they are made, there
will be a simple invitation

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:51.180
to those who buy into that idea
to leave to us the fighting.

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:54.650
So if you've been drafted, if
you're here against your will,

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:56.210
we'll ask you to leave.

00:28:56.210 --> 00:29:00.080
Jackson, when he was told
by the Secretary of War

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:04.430
that he must federalize
his troops, he refused.

00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:06.560
And he said, this is
not what I promised

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:08.555
the parents of these young men.

00:29:08.555 --> 00:29:11.960
These young men are here
strictly because they

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:14.630
believe the fight is correct.

00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:18.020
And he refused to follow the
order of John Armstrong, then

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:19.610
the Secretary of War.

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:23.730
And he said, I would remind
you of your own army's success.

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.510
Was it not under
the US Army Command

00:29:26.510 --> 00:29:30.080
of William Hull
at Detroit in 1812

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:33.320
that you surrendered a third
of our forces to the British?

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:35.300
Where is your glorious army?

00:29:35.300 --> 00:29:39.650
And by what right do you
have to regulate my militia?

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:43.130
He says, "A nobler feeling
should impel us to action.

00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:44.150
Who are we?

00:29:44.150 --> 00:29:45.830
What are we going to fight for?

00:29:45.830 --> 00:29:50.300
Are we the slaves of George
III--" this is in 1813.

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:53.840
"Are we the slaves of George
III or the military conscripts

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:57.710
of Napoleon or the frozen
peasants of the Russian czar?

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:58.520
No.

00:29:58.520 --> 00:30:02.360
We are freeborn sons of
the Republic of America,

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:04.470
the citizens of
the only republic

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:07.460
now existing in the world and
the only people on earth who

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:10.310
possess rights,
liberty, and property

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:13.930
which they dare call their own."

00:30:13.930 --> 00:30:15.226
It's amazing.

00:30:15.226 --> 00:30:16.570
And think about that.

00:30:16.570 --> 00:30:19.120
And I hope-- this is one
of those things we rarely

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:21.490
teach in our classrooms.

00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:24.070
I hope we have to
understand and explain

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:25.910
that this is a different world.

00:30:25.910 --> 00:30:28.360
And when Jackson is
against the military,

00:30:28.360 --> 00:30:31.450
in this kind of audience that I
have right here in front of me,

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:33.100
this is odd to us.

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:35.920
But when we imagine what the
history of standing armies

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:38.530
had been at the time
that Jackson speaks

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:42.250
in 1812 and 1813, we
can understand why

00:30:42.250 --> 00:30:44.390
he's so hesitant about this.

00:30:44.390 --> 00:30:46.480
But he takes this even further.

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:51.220
When he is inaugurated
on March 4 of 1829,

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:55.030
he will not allow any military
officers to be present.

00:30:55.030 --> 00:30:57.460
And if they're present,
they may not be in uniform.

00:30:57.460 --> 00:31:01.270
But he does invite all the
militias of the present day

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:04.360
and of all those who fought
during the Revolutionary War

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:05.800
to come and parade.

00:31:05.800 --> 00:31:08.170
And he walks with them
during his inaugural.

00:31:08.170 --> 00:31:11.620
That's astounding in
all kinds of ways.

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:14.260
OK, point number four.

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:17.410
Well, despite this,
Andrew Jackson

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:20.860
is one of the greatest
victors in US history.

00:31:20.860 --> 00:31:25.690
And we can think about what
he did on January 8 of 1815.

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:29.410
And I would remind everyone
here that Jackson's force was

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:33.190
a force of only
2,000 that he had

00:31:33.190 --> 00:31:34.670
at the Battle of New Orleans.

00:31:34.670 --> 00:31:37.840
And it was Jackson
almost alone who

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:40.300
understood that
after the British had

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:44.620
invaded in the Great Lakes
and then up the Chesapeake,

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:49.480
their next venue
would be Mississippi.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:52.250
Very few people believed that
was going to be the case.

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:55.480
We were in the middle of
signing a peace in the fall

00:31:55.480 --> 00:31:58.330
and early winter of
1814 with Britain.

00:31:58.330 --> 00:32:00.820
Britain had just
concluded hostilities

00:32:00.820 --> 00:32:02.650
by defeating Napoleon.

00:32:02.650 --> 00:32:04.510
And we thought this was it.

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:06.050
Jackson knew better.

00:32:06.050 --> 00:32:09.580
He knew that the British
would do one last thing.

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:12.580
We also know this, that when
the British were given orders

00:32:12.580 --> 00:32:16.870
and there were 14,000 troops
being carried on 60 ships

00:32:16.870 --> 00:32:20.200
under the leadership of Ned
Pakenham coming from Britain,

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:23.800
these were all Napoleonic
soldiers battle-hardened.

00:32:23.800 --> 00:32:25.270
These were not draftees.

00:32:25.270 --> 00:32:27.040
These were not new recruits.

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:30.970
These were men who had
seen much action in Europe

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:32.950
throughout the Napoleonic Wars.

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:36.460
And they had express
permission from both Parliament

00:32:36.460 --> 00:32:39.640
and the king that, when
they were to attack,

00:32:39.640 --> 00:32:42.220
it was not to be
a harassment raid.

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:45.340
It was to be an attack
to reclaim property

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:47.590
all along the Mississippi River.

00:32:47.590 --> 00:32:50.230
That's something we often
forget about in history.

00:32:50.230 --> 00:32:52.810
This was not meant to
be some harassment run.

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:56.080
The British were here for
blood and for our soil.

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:57.700
And Jackson understood this.

00:32:57.700 --> 00:32:59.570
He knew that this was coming.

00:32:59.570 --> 00:33:02.680
And so he, in what would
resemble modern trench

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:06.110
warfare, what we will see at
Petersburg during the Civil War

00:33:06.110 --> 00:33:08.590
or what we'll see at
the Battle of the Somme

00:33:08.590 --> 00:33:11.200
and other great
battles in World War I,

00:33:11.200 --> 00:33:14.600
He created massive trenches
waiting for the British

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:15.100
to come.

00:33:15.100 --> 00:33:17.110
And again, I'll remind
us of that force.

00:33:17.110 --> 00:33:20.740
Jackson has just a
little over 2,000 men--

00:33:20.740 --> 00:33:22.330
all recruits, all free.

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:25.880
He refused to allow any
draftees with him at all.

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:29.410
He would find that abhorrent.

00:33:29.410 --> 00:33:32.890
So he has only about
2,000 men, but they

00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:37.360
are in great battle-hardened
mode behind these trenches

00:33:37.360 --> 00:33:39.205
in these dugouts.

00:33:39.205 --> 00:33:40.705
Well, one of my
favorite historians,

00:33:40.705 --> 00:33:42.880
who was actually a
journalist before he became

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:46.360
a historian, Paul Johnson,
writes in his excellent book

00:33:46.360 --> 00:33:49.420
from 1991, The Birth
of The Modern--

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:54.430
he describes how terrible
the British were in executing

00:33:54.430 --> 00:33:57.550
this plan at New Orleans.

00:33:57.550 --> 00:34:01.870
He says, "The Great Duke
of Wellington, Pakenham,

00:34:01.870 --> 00:34:04.060
had married his
sister Catherine,

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:06.580
but that was the closest
that that man had ever

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:09.219
gotten to any military mastery.

00:34:09.219 --> 00:34:12.670
"Money and connections had
made Pakenham a Major before he

00:34:12.670 --> 00:34:16.090
was 17 and a Colonel at age 21.

00:34:16.090 --> 00:34:18.250
There was no question
of his courage.

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:21.520
Serving as a Major General under
his brother-in-law at Salamanca

00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:24.190
in 1812, he had been
given the chance

00:34:24.190 --> 00:34:27.280
when Wellington told him to
take his division straight

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:31.960
at the French center with the
words, now is your time, Ned.

00:34:31.960 --> 00:34:33.610
Ned broke through
the French line.

00:34:33.610 --> 00:34:37.219
And this, Wellington said in
a dispatch, won the battle,

00:34:37.219 --> 00:34:40.239
though he admitted, quote,
'Pakenham may not be

00:34:40.239 --> 00:34:41.440
the brightest of geniuses.'"

00:34:41.440 --> 00:34:44.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:48.750
He launched a full frontal
assault on Jackson's position

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:52.139
on January 8 of 1815.

00:34:52.139 --> 00:34:56.610
Andrew Jackson in his own
report says that he had about 10

00:34:56.610 --> 00:35:01.710
of his own men killed and
about 10 wounded total--

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:04.590
20 casualties out of 2,000.

00:35:04.590 --> 00:35:11.070
The British that day lost
1,500 Marines and another 500

00:35:11.070 --> 00:35:12.952
who were taken prisoner.

00:35:12.952 --> 00:35:14.740
Imagine that.

00:35:14.740 --> 00:35:18.370
There were close to
44 bodies that were so

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:21.520
decimated in the Gulf of
Mexico that they could not

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:23.350
find the remains of them.

00:35:23.350 --> 00:35:24.880
They couldn't identify.

00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:26.600
This is incredible.

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:28.930
And what do we have and
what comes out of this?

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:30.910
And this is where,
no matter what,

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:35.260
even if we remembered Andrew
Jackson for nothing else,

00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:38.110
he is the hero of New Orleans.

00:35:38.110 --> 00:35:39.250
Was he lucky?

00:35:39.250 --> 00:35:40.030
Maybe.

00:35:40.030 --> 00:35:41.020
Was he smart?

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:41.740
Maybe.

00:35:41.740 --> 00:35:42.970
Did he win?

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:45.100
He won, brutally.

00:35:45.100 --> 00:35:46.360
He won.

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:48.370
And so what does
Paul Johnson say?

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:50.380
Paul Johnson, who
is British himself,

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:56.660
says, this was the great moment
in Anglo-American relations.

00:35:56.660 --> 00:36:00.130
It was at this moment alone
that the British finally

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:02.680
took us seriously as a country.

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:07.360
It was at this moment that the
great Anglo-American Alliance,

00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:10.240
which had lasted, and
still does up until 2020--

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:14.050
but certainly at the time that
Johnson wrote this in 1991,

00:36:14.050 --> 00:36:18.460
this was the beginning of our
mutual respect with each other.

00:36:18.460 --> 00:36:20.680
And you think about
Andrew Jackson.

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:23.150
He's now as great
as William Wallace.

00:36:23.150 --> 00:36:25.510
He's now as great as James Wolf.

00:36:25.510 --> 00:36:28.450
He is truly one of
the great generals

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:30.550
in history at that point.

00:36:30.550 --> 00:36:34.000
And more than any
other title he had,

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:35.650
the title that meant
the most to him

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:38.140
and the one that meant
the most to Americans,

00:36:38.140 --> 00:36:42.190
always, was that he was
the hero of New Orleans.

00:36:42.190 --> 00:36:43.860
Finally, point number five.

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:47.560
And I'll conclude with this, and
we'll open it up for questions.

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:49.690
One of my favorite things
about Andrew Jackson--

00:36:49.690 --> 00:36:54.150
so today-- you'll have
to forgive me for this--

00:36:54.150 --> 00:36:57.900
but I wore-- and I don't know
how many of you can see this.

00:36:57.900 --> 00:37:01.180
My grandfather, who was
the best man I ever knew,

00:37:01.180 --> 00:37:02.860
he died in 1982.

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:07.800
But I wore his campaign button
for Dwight D Eisenhower from--

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:09.690
I don't know if it's
from '52 or '56,

00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:12.130
but it's from one
of those elections.

00:37:12.130 --> 00:37:13.247
And I've had it with me.

00:37:13.247 --> 00:37:14.330
I've never worn it before.

00:37:14.330 --> 00:37:15.990
This is the first
time I've worn it.

00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:18.840
And I thought, we're here
to talk about character.

00:37:18.840 --> 00:37:22.030
We're here to talk
about American generals.

00:37:22.030 --> 00:37:25.200
What better figure other than
Andrew Jackson than Dwight D.

00:37:25.200 --> 00:37:26.480
Eisenhower?

00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:28.470
What a great thing.

00:37:28.470 --> 00:37:30.600
And I grew up in Kansas.

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:32.770
I've been to the Eisenhower
museum a million times.

00:37:32.770 --> 00:37:34.228
So you guys will
please forgive me.

00:37:34.228 --> 00:37:36.960
I've already given you
Jackson's views on the military.

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:39.480
And they're probably
not so well taken,

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:42.743
necessarily, in this audience
and understandably so.

00:37:42.743 --> 00:37:44.160
But I would also
give another view

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:46.860
of Andrew Jackson and something
that I think we often forget,

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:48.330
but should remember.

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:50.850
At the end of Andrew Jackson's
life-- he died in 1845,

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:52.920
as I mentioned earlier--

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:58.300
people, his friends, offered
to build him a mausoleum so

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:01.870
that they could dedicate at
least some memorial to him

00:38:01.870 --> 00:38:05.860
as hero of New Orleans and as
President of the United States.

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:07.930
And Jackson writes them back.

00:38:07.930 --> 00:38:11.050
And he says, "I am
so honored that you

00:38:11.050 --> 00:38:13.780
would want to remember
me after my death,

00:38:13.780 --> 00:38:16.870
but I must decline any memorial.

00:38:16.870 --> 00:38:21.160
My own republican feelings and
my principles of republicanism

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:22.760
forbid it.

00:38:22.760 --> 00:38:25.600
And the simplicity of our
way of life as a republic

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:27.160
should forbid it.

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:32.050
True virtue cannot exist where
pomp and parade are governing

00:38:32.050 --> 00:38:34.330
passions.

00:38:34.330 --> 00:38:37.630
It can only dwell
with the people--

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:40.660
the great laboring
and producing classes

00:38:40.660 --> 00:38:45.130
that form the bone and the
sinew of our republic."

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:46.424
Thank you very much.

00:38:46.424 --> 00:38:49.882
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:57.786 --> 00:38:59.690
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you, Dr. Birzer.

00:38:59.690 --> 00:39:01.815
BRADLEY J BIRZER: You're
welcome, wherever you are.

00:39:01.815 --> 00:39:02.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:03.630
I really can't see.

00:39:03.630 --> 00:39:04.520
SPEAKER 2: I'm right over here.

00:39:04.520 --> 00:39:05.180
BRADLEY J BIRZER:
Oh, there you are.

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:05.990
Thank you.

00:39:05.990 --> 00:39:08.093
SPEAKER 2: We now have
time for Q&amp;A. Please

00:39:08.093 --> 00:39:10.385
make your way to the microphone
if you have a question.

00:39:24.812 --> 00:39:26.520
AUDIENCE: Doctor,
could you say something

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:28.183
about the Bank of
the United States?

00:39:28.183 --> 00:39:30.600
BRADLEY J BIRZER: I'm sorry
could you repeat that, please?

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:32.100
AUDIENCE: Could you say
something about Jackson

00:39:32.100 --> 00:39:33.510
and the Bank of
the United States?

00:39:33.510 --> 00:39:34.635
BRADLEY J BIRZER: Oh, sure.

00:39:34.635 --> 00:39:36.150
Yeah.

00:39:36.150 --> 00:39:38.700
So the question about Jackson
and the Bank of the United

00:39:38.700 --> 00:39:39.800
States is a great one.

00:39:39.800 --> 00:39:41.550
Because obviously--
and it's not something

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:44.130
I focused on, since we're
doing with military history,

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:45.690
but it's related.

00:39:45.690 --> 00:39:48.080
Andrew Jackson in his honesty--

00:39:48.080 --> 00:39:49.830
and I think we could
probably debate this,

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:52.860
but people at the Mises
Institute and Ron Paul

00:39:52.860 --> 00:39:54.330
would agree with this.

00:39:54.330 --> 00:39:56.580
Jackson's argument
was essentially

00:39:56.580 --> 00:39:59.550
that money must be real.

00:39:59.550 --> 00:40:01.970
If it's not actual
specie, that is,

00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:06.060
if it's not based on a
physical commodity, it's a lie.

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:08.690
This was part of
Jackson's honesty.

00:40:08.690 --> 00:40:10.500
He was a gold man in every way.

00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:13.290
Gold, silver, whatever it is
that we wanted to trade in,

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:15.010
but it had to be a commodity.

00:40:15.010 --> 00:40:18.540
And so from the very beginning
of the First Bank of the United

00:40:18.540 --> 00:40:21.510
States, Jackson had
vehemently opposed it.

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:24.330
When he was a young man,
he opposed the Second Bank

00:40:24.330 --> 00:40:25.512
of the United States.

00:40:25.512 --> 00:40:26.970
And of course, when
he's president,

00:40:26.970 --> 00:40:29.500
he can actually do
something about this.

00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:32.190
And when the Whigs
tried to outmaneuver him

00:40:32.190 --> 00:40:35.820
during his first administration
and have the bank come up

00:40:35.820 --> 00:40:39.270
four years early for renewal,
for a 20-year renewal,

00:40:39.270 --> 00:40:42.490
Jackson uses this to
solidify his own power.

00:40:42.490 --> 00:40:44.880
In fact, it in some
ways, Jackson really

00:40:44.880 --> 00:40:48.300
becomes Jackson as
president after his victory

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:50.550
against the Second Bank
of the United States.

00:40:50.550 --> 00:40:51.720
And he makes two arguments.

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:54.870
He says, number one,
ultimately, this is a lie.

00:40:54.870 --> 00:40:57.750
Paper money is always
and everywhere a lie.

00:40:57.750 --> 00:40:59.670
Even if you could
have paper money

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:02.070
effective for a
certain amount of time,

00:41:02.070 --> 00:41:05.370
it will always become corrupt
at some point, because it's not

00:41:05.370 --> 00:41:06.490
worth anything.

00:41:06.490 --> 00:41:08.760
It's only worth what
we state it's worth.

00:41:08.760 --> 00:41:11.850
And therefore, it can't
have any longevity.

00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:13.920
But the second point
he made to this

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:15.600
was a very interesting
one, and one I

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:17.880
think that those of us
who love the free market

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:19.020
should remember.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:23.340
His argument was that power
rarely benefits the poor.

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:25.830
It almost always
benefits the rich.

00:41:25.830 --> 00:41:29.700
And what was the Second Bank of
the United States founded for?

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:33.270
Not to create equality among
the peoples of America,

00:41:33.270 --> 00:41:35.250
but to give privilege
to the wealthy.

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:38.400
And he actually argued through
much of his own arguments

00:41:38.400 --> 00:41:41.610
on government privilege that
that's the thing that we most

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:42.930
be concerned about.

00:41:42.930 --> 00:41:44.190
The poor will always be here.

00:41:44.190 --> 00:41:45.520
They will come and go.

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:47.910
But they'll never really
have any long-term say

00:41:47.910 --> 00:41:50.370
over the government, he
thought, but the rich would.

00:41:50.370 --> 00:41:52.180
And we had to be
very careful of that.

00:41:52.180 --> 00:41:55.590
But he's coming from a radically
laissez-faire argument on that,

00:41:55.590 --> 00:41:57.390
which I find very interesting.

00:41:57.390 --> 00:41:59.496
So thank you for that question.

00:41:59.496 --> 00:42:03.158
AUDIENCE: Can you comment
on the Trail of Tears?

00:42:03.158 --> 00:42:04.950
BRADLEY J BIRZER: Is
that coming from here?

00:42:04.950 --> 00:42:07.283
SPEAKER 2: Sorry, the question
is to the speaker's left.

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:12.690
GORDON AUSTIN: Professor,
my name is Gordon Austin.

00:42:12.690 --> 00:42:15.360
I'm a retired Naval officer
and I came from Georgia

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:17.073
specifically to
hear this series.

00:42:17.073 --> 00:42:18.990
And I want to compliment
you on getting it off

00:42:18.990 --> 00:42:19.923
to such a good start.

00:42:19.923 --> 00:42:21.090
BRADLEY J BIRZER: Thank you.

00:42:21.090 --> 00:42:23.132
GORDON AUSTIN: You alluded
to what my question is

00:42:23.132 --> 00:42:24.120
at the beginning.

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:25.920
I recently visited
the Hermitage.

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:30.330
And I was struck by the
comparison between Donald Trump

00:42:30.330 --> 00:42:33.900
and Andrew Jackson, especially
in contemporary descriptions

00:42:33.900 --> 00:42:34.980
of Jackson.

00:42:34.980 --> 00:42:36.512
Could you expand on that?

00:42:36.512 --> 00:42:37.470
BRADLEY J BIRZER: Sure.

00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:40.140
So one of the reasons
that I did write my book

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:43.170
was simply because there was
so much interest in Trump

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:45.630
being interested in Jackson.

00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:48.330
And if you go back and
look-- so Donald Trump

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:51.567
gave his speech on Andrew
Jackson's 250th birthday.

00:42:51.567 --> 00:42:52.650
And it's actually one of--

00:42:52.650 --> 00:42:56.507
I think one of Trump's more
really well done speeches.

00:42:56.507 --> 00:42:57.840
I don't think it would compare--

00:42:57.840 --> 00:43:01.080
the one that he gave at the
Right to Life March was--

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:02.880
if we remember him
for anything, it

00:43:02.880 --> 00:43:05.100
will be for that speech,
which was probably

00:43:05.100 --> 00:43:09.120
written by a Hillsdale graduate,
Brittany Baldwin, most likely.

00:43:09.120 --> 00:43:11.520
She is his speechwriter
on all things religious.

00:43:11.520 --> 00:43:12.750
So I assume that--

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:15.330
she can't take credit for that,
so I will give her credit.

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:17.400
I'm pretty sure she's
the one who wrote that.

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:18.570
And amen.

00:43:18.570 --> 00:43:20.430
But the speech he
gave on Jackson

00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:21.990
was really quite interesting.

00:43:21.990 --> 00:43:25.410
Now it's three years old,
coming up on three years.

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:27.040
And there've been
a lot of questions

00:43:27.040 --> 00:43:28.650
about what's the similarity.

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:33.060
And most of the people who are
commenting on the similarities

00:43:33.060 --> 00:43:37.110
have done so because they both
come at these populist moments,

00:43:37.110 --> 00:43:40.230
these moments where people
are trying to take back what's

00:43:40.230 --> 00:43:43.350
going on in Washington, DC.

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:46.690
There's no question-- I can't
speak as effectively on Trump.

00:43:46.690 --> 00:43:49.890
There is no doubt that when
Andrew Jackson went to DC,

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:51.420
he cleaned the place up.

00:43:51.420 --> 00:43:54.540
Andrew Jackson actually
has a squeaky clean record.

00:43:54.540 --> 00:43:56.290
We may not always agree
with his policies.

00:43:56.290 --> 00:43:58.123
I heard someone yell
out the Trail of Tears.

00:43:58.123 --> 00:44:00.330
And there's no real
defensive of that.

00:44:00.330 --> 00:44:02.370
There's an explanation,
but not a defense of it.

00:44:02.370 --> 00:44:03.660
It's heinous.

00:44:03.660 --> 00:44:09.520
But when we look at his actual
practices, he's squeaky clean.

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:11.310
We don't have
examples of corruption

00:44:11.310 --> 00:44:13.560
in his administration at all.

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:15.660
And I mean, there
were things-- like, he

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:18.810
put in charge of all the
bureaucrats in Washington,

00:44:18.810 --> 00:44:21.600
he put a guy in charge by
the name of Amos Kendall.

00:44:21.600 --> 00:44:25.210
And Kendall used to walk
around from office to office.

00:44:25.210 --> 00:44:28.380
And if he thought anybody
was wasting government time,

00:44:28.380 --> 00:44:31.440
if they were reading
a newspaper or a novel

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:34.380
or if they were just
sitting there, he fired him.

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:35.430
You can't do this.

00:44:35.430 --> 00:44:36.720
You're on the people's dime.

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:38.610
You're working or you're out.

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:41.970
And there's no doubt
Jackson spent his eight

00:44:41.970 --> 00:44:44.920
years in Washington
cleaning things out.

00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:46.890
And if I remember
correctly, he's

00:44:46.890 --> 00:44:48.810
the only president
outside of Clinton

00:44:48.810 --> 00:44:51.540
to have fully
balanced the budget.

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:53.980
And he was very
effective at this.

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:57.000
And a lot of it had to do with
these policies of corruption.

00:44:57.000 --> 00:45:00.200
I don't think Trump has
been quite as effective,

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:01.510
but there are reasons for that.

00:45:01.510 --> 00:45:03.960
The bureaucracy is
much deeper than it

00:45:03.960 --> 00:45:05.340
was under Andrew Jackson.

00:45:05.340 --> 00:45:07.050
Things were still
very politicized

00:45:07.050 --> 00:45:08.358
under Andrew Jackson.

00:45:08.358 --> 00:45:10.900
You either were the follower of
the president or you weren't.

00:45:10.900 --> 00:45:12.630
And if you weren't,
you lost your job.

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:16.080
And it didn't matter if you
were in DC or a postal carrier,

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:17.290
you were out.

00:45:17.290 --> 00:45:20.010
And so there was no
civil service tenure

00:45:20.010 --> 00:45:21.640
other than what
the president said,

00:45:21.640 --> 00:45:23.283
if you're in an
executive office.

00:45:23.283 --> 00:45:25.450
The other thing that they
have in common, of course,

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:26.940
is they're both Scottish.

00:45:26.940 --> 00:45:29.070
And they have that
kind of tendency,

00:45:29.070 --> 00:45:33.150
that tendency to speak
of the cuff at times.

00:45:33.150 --> 00:45:35.348
They also are
interesting-- and I

00:45:35.348 --> 00:45:37.140
don't want to get too
political about this,

00:45:37.140 --> 00:45:38.760
but, you know, when
it comes to women,

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:40.770
they both have interesting
relations with women.

00:45:40.770 --> 00:45:41.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:41.490 --> 00:45:44.670
But I would say Jackson's
were a lot more traditional

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:46.320
than Trump's have been.

00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:50.240
So I don't want to get too much
more into detail about that,

00:45:50.240 --> 00:45:51.210
but yeah.

00:45:51.210 --> 00:45:52.940
So I don't know if
that answers you.

00:45:52.940 --> 00:45:55.110
But their policies aren't that--

00:45:55.110 --> 00:45:56.952
they're not totally
that similar.

00:45:56.952 --> 00:45:58.410
I mean, when we
think about Jackson

00:45:58.410 --> 00:46:03.690
being so anti-military and
so pro-free trade and so

00:46:03.690 --> 00:46:04.320
laissez-faire.

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:06.910
I think Trump's been really
good on the laissez-faire part,

00:46:06.910 --> 00:46:09.470
especially with the
regulatory offices.

00:46:09.470 --> 00:46:10.470
But those other things--

00:46:10.470 --> 00:46:12.360
I mean, he's clearly
not been free trade

00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:14.190
by any means, good or bad.

00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:17.110
He's different from
Jackson in that way.

00:46:17.110 --> 00:46:21.440
So thank you very
much for a question.

00:46:21.440 --> 00:46:22.930
AUDIENCE: Over here.

00:46:22.930 --> 00:46:24.983
SPEAKER 2: Question
to the speaker.

00:46:24.983 --> 00:46:26.150
BRADLEY J BIRZER: Thank you.

00:46:26.150 --> 00:46:29.060
AUDIENCE: Thanks very
much for your talk.

00:46:29.060 --> 00:46:30.740
In your introductory
remarks, you

00:46:30.740 --> 00:46:32.840
talked about the
reputation of Jackson

00:46:32.840 --> 00:46:34.840
being a rapist and
an Indian murderer.

00:46:34.840 --> 00:46:37.760
And I really know
nothing about Jackson.

00:46:37.760 --> 00:46:40.280
But could you talk about--
where did those stories

00:46:40.280 --> 00:46:42.073
come from if they weren't true?

00:46:42.073 --> 00:46:43.490
BRADLEY J BIRZER:
So a lot of them

00:46:43.490 --> 00:46:45.810
came from his political
opponents at the time.

00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:48.150
They used to report
things like this.

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:50.150
They'd have Jackson
for president,

00:46:50.150 --> 00:46:52.580
do you want a murderer
in the White House--

00:46:52.580 --> 00:46:53.620
things like that.

00:46:53.620 --> 00:46:54.620
But we do remember them.

00:46:54.620 --> 00:46:57.370
And there's no easy
defense of this.

00:46:57.370 --> 00:47:00.440
I mean, he clearly is
responsible for the removal

00:47:00.440 --> 00:47:03.050
of thousands upon
thousands of Indians

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:05.510
from their traditional
homelands to the west.

00:47:05.510 --> 00:47:06.620
And they were removed.

00:47:06.620 --> 00:47:09.380
If they were from
the southern states,

00:47:09.380 --> 00:47:11.090
they were removed to Kansas--

00:47:11.090 --> 00:47:12.350
excuse me, Oklahoma.

00:47:12.350 --> 00:47:14.270
And from the northern
places, like here,

00:47:14.270 --> 00:47:17.060
Hillsdale, when the
Potawatomis were moved,

00:47:17.060 --> 00:47:19.310
they were moved to
Kansas around Topeka.

00:47:19.310 --> 00:47:21.020
And he is responsible for that.

00:47:21.020 --> 00:47:23.360
Now, we can look
at his arguments.

00:47:23.360 --> 00:47:26.270
He did believe very
strongly that removal

00:47:26.270 --> 00:47:30.020
was a preferable option to
keeping them where they were

00:47:30.020 --> 00:47:31.520
where, where they
would keep getting

00:47:31.520 --> 00:47:35.780
surrounded and out-populated
by those of European descent.

00:47:35.780 --> 00:47:39.320
But the execution of
the removal was heinous.

00:47:39.320 --> 00:47:42.170
When we look, for example,
at the Choctaw Indians, one

00:47:42.170 --> 00:47:44.750
of the five major
tribes of the South

00:47:44.750 --> 00:47:49.310
that was removed to Oklahoma,
when they were removed in 1831,

00:47:49.310 --> 00:47:54.920
in 1832, they started off with
18,000 people in their tribe.

00:47:54.920 --> 00:47:57.620
A year later, they
were down to 6,000.

00:47:57.620 --> 00:48:00.500
They lost 2/3 of their
people in their removal.

00:48:00.500 --> 00:48:02.730
There's no defense of that.

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:06.230
I think a lot of it had to do
with poor execution rather than

00:48:06.230 --> 00:48:07.880
actual intent of the policy.

00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:12.260
But regardless, Jackson has to
be held accountable for that,

00:48:12.260 --> 00:48:13.610
at some level.

00:48:13.610 --> 00:48:15.620
We have to be able
to recognize that.

00:48:15.620 --> 00:48:17.720
Now, interestingly enough,
I mean, those people

00:48:17.720 --> 00:48:20.730
who survived, if you
look at Indian tribes,

00:48:20.730 --> 00:48:23.180
the Choctaws became the
single wealthiest Indian tribe

00:48:23.180 --> 00:48:25.370
by the 1850s and 1860s.

00:48:25.370 --> 00:48:28.430
But then, of course, they sided
with the Confederacy and--

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:32.100
you know, this stuff is
very, very complicated.

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:36.080
In fact, more Indians
owned slaves per capita

00:48:36.080 --> 00:48:38.630
than White Americans did.

00:48:38.630 --> 00:48:41.810
We never say that to
our children, right?

00:48:41.810 --> 00:48:44.160
And I'm not trying to
condemn anyone in particular.

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:47.250
It just makes the story
really, really complicated.

00:48:47.250 --> 00:48:49.310
There's no evidence of the rape.

00:48:49.310 --> 00:48:50.570
I actually was--

00:48:50.570 --> 00:48:52.640
I was on a radio show,
one of the first.

00:48:52.640 --> 00:48:53.235
And I blew it.

00:48:53.235 --> 00:48:54.110
I did a horrible job.

00:48:54.110 --> 00:48:54.735
If any of you--

00:48:54.735 --> 00:48:57.560
[LAUGHS] if any of you want
to see me do a terrible job

00:48:57.560 --> 00:49:00.900
at something-- and hopefully
I didn't tonight on this--

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:02.420
if you get on--
just go onto YouTube

00:49:02.420 --> 00:49:04.820
and do "Brad Birzer
interview Andrew Jackson."

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:05.960
This will pop up.

00:49:05.960 --> 00:49:07.640
And I was horrible at it.

00:49:07.640 --> 00:49:08.440
This guy came up.

00:49:08.440 --> 00:49:09.857
And he said well,
Andrew Jackson's

00:49:09.857 --> 00:49:10.830
a rapist and all this.

00:49:10.830 --> 00:49:11.975
And I was so taken aback.

00:49:11.975 --> 00:49:15.450
Here I'd spent all this
time writing this book.

00:49:15.450 --> 00:49:16.670
I was like, what?

00:49:16.670 --> 00:49:17.940
I'd not heard any of this.

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:22.040
So I did a quick search
of "Andrew Jackson rapist"

00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:23.300
on Google.

00:49:23.300 --> 00:49:26.060
And the eight of
the first 10 results

00:49:26.060 --> 00:49:29.750
were of a rape in a public
school called the Andrew

00:49:29.750 --> 00:49:32.630
Jackson School in Georgia.

00:49:32.630 --> 00:49:35.750
And I don't know if
that's where it came from,

00:49:35.750 --> 00:49:38.990
but I'm guessing, because there
was nothing in his lifetime

00:49:38.990 --> 00:49:42.030
where anyone ever
suggested that at all.

00:49:42.030 --> 00:49:44.270
In fact, if anything,
people made fun of him

00:49:44.270 --> 00:49:47.100
for being so taken
with his wife.

00:49:47.100 --> 00:49:49.910
They didn't-- actually, it was
one of those few things where

00:49:49.910 --> 00:49:52.130
they thought Jackson
wasn't manly enough,

00:49:52.130 --> 00:49:54.740
because his wife
commanded him around.

00:49:54.740 --> 00:49:58.580
And she didn't command him, but
they were definitely equals.

00:49:58.580 --> 00:49:59.270
No question.

00:49:59.270 --> 00:50:01.830
And I wouldn't have
messed with her, either.

00:50:01.830 --> 00:50:03.620
She was a fierce person--

00:50:03.620 --> 00:50:08.520
devout, hardcore, Bible-thumping
Presbyterian from day one.

00:50:08.520 --> 00:50:11.020
But you know, I
think she's amazing.

00:50:11.020 --> 00:50:13.720
And I'm shocked there
aren't more books about her.

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:16.160
And she was very liberally
educated, actually,

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:17.330
and a great writer.

00:50:17.330 --> 00:50:18.827
So I can't believe--

00:50:18.827 --> 00:50:20.910
I don't know why people
haven't written about her.

00:50:20.910 --> 00:50:21.920
They should.

00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:22.880
She's a great person.

00:50:22.880 --> 00:50:24.450
So I hope that answers you.

00:50:24.450 --> 00:50:25.523
Yeah, thank you.

00:50:25.523 --> 00:50:27.440
SPEAKER 2: We have time
for one more question.

00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:29.270
BRADLEY J BIRZER: Sure.

00:50:29.270 --> 00:50:30.830
AUDIENCE: Thank you, Dr. Birzer.

00:50:30.830 --> 00:50:35.870
My question is-- it's about
General Jackson rallying troops

00:50:35.870 --> 00:50:40.460
to defend New Orleans,
with the recent purchase

00:50:40.460 --> 00:50:43.970
of the Louisiana Territory
under the Jefferson presidency.

00:50:43.970 --> 00:50:46.460
What did General
Jackson have to do

00:50:46.460 --> 00:50:50.900
to win the support of the locals
of New Orleans, especially

00:50:50.900 --> 00:50:52.450
with the change in loyalties?

00:50:52.450 --> 00:50:53.450
BRADLEY J BIRZER: Right.

00:50:53.450 --> 00:50:55.140
So you know, it's
very interesting.

00:50:55.140 --> 00:50:56.970
We don't-- there
are a lot of things.

00:50:56.970 --> 00:50:59.490
We have a lot of good evidence
about what he did there.

00:50:59.490 --> 00:51:02.420
And I would really recommend--
in my book on Jackson,

00:51:02.420 --> 00:51:05.042
I only spend about
four pages on this.

00:51:05.042 --> 00:51:07.250
I think it's really important,
but I just kind of get

00:51:07.250 --> 00:51:08.810
at the mythical element.

00:51:08.810 --> 00:51:11.900
If any of you are interested,
this Fox News correspondent,

00:51:11.900 --> 00:51:16.430
Brian Kilmeade, has written a
book on Jackson at New Orleans.

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:18.950
And I think it's
an excellent book.

00:51:18.950 --> 00:51:21.740
It is very well researched
and beautifully written.

00:51:21.740 --> 00:51:24.080
And he deals exactly with this.

00:51:24.080 --> 00:51:25.302
So I don't want to defer--

00:51:25.302 --> 00:51:27.010
I don't want to not
answer your question,

00:51:27.010 --> 00:51:29.530
but really, if you
want to look at that,

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:32.710
I think he's probably the best
of current writers dealing

00:51:32.710 --> 00:51:33.370
with that.

00:51:33.370 --> 00:51:35.170
But Jackson, I mean,
there is no doubt

00:51:35.170 --> 00:51:37.600
that there was some opposition
to what he was doing,

00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:38.950
especially--

00:51:38.950 --> 00:51:40.990
Jackson, he walked into town.

00:51:40.990 --> 00:51:42.595
It's basically his town.

00:51:42.595 --> 00:51:44.980
He comes in with his
militia, and they're not

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:45.990
even from that area.

00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:47.980
Remember, these are
Tennessee guys, right?

00:51:47.980 --> 00:51:51.290
But he's able to recruit
some people within the area.

00:51:51.290 --> 00:51:53.920
But he mostly tells the
citizens of New Orleans

00:51:53.920 --> 00:51:55.150
just to stay away.

00:51:55.150 --> 00:51:58.600
He doesn't want them near the
battle, on his side or out.

00:51:58.600 --> 00:52:01.190
He wants his men,
whom he can trust.

00:52:01.190 --> 00:52:03.190
And by the way, just to
get to the question that

00:52:03.190 --> 00:52:05.690
was asked a moment ago,
it is very interesting.

00:52:05.690 --> 00:52:07.510
Andrew Jackson's
army was made up--

00:52:07.510 --> 00:52:09.760
it was mostly white Tennesseans.

00:52:09.760 --> 00:52:13.600
But about a third of it was
freed Blacks and Indians,

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:16.150
who were his allies.

00:52:16.150 --> 00:52:18.940
Again, it's one of those things
we don't often talk about.

00:52:18.940 --> 00:52:19.880
It's a motley crew.

00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:22.840
It's an interesting-- and he
even recruits some pirates very

00:52:22.840 --> 00:52:23.350
famously--

00:52:23.350 --> 00:52:28.020
John La Feet from the area--
who decide to fight with him.

00:52:28.020 --> 00:52:29.560
And do it very success--

00:52:29.560 --> 00:52:31.420
they're very successful at this.

00:52:31.420 --> 00:52:34.030
But Jackson more or less puts--

00:52:34.030 --> 00:52:35.710
it's not official,
but he more or less

00:52:35.710 --> 00:52:38.150
puts New Orleans under
martial law during the time.

00:52:38.150 --> 00:52:39.250
He can't regulate it.

00:52:39.250 --> 00:52:41.960
It's something he just has
to depend that they'll do.

00:52:41.960 --> 00:52:44.500
But he basically tells
them, don't bother with us,

00:52:44.500 --> 00:52:46.120
we won't bother with you.

00:52:46.120 --> 00:52:49.210
And of all the people-- there
are crazy stories that come out

00:52:49.210 --> 00:52:51.310
of this miracle at New Orleans.

00:52:51.310 --> 00:52:53.200
But one group of--

00:52:53.200 --> 00:52:56.320
if you think about it,
these are French and Spanish

00:52:56.320 --> 00:52:57.790
who are living there.

00:52:57.790 --> 00:52:59.230
The people who
help him the most,

00:52:59.230 --> 00:53:03.160
there's a convent of nuns
there who, for whatever reason,

00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:05.620
think Andrew Jackson
is this great guy.

00:53:05.620 --> 00:53:07.900
And I'm guessing he
treated them pretty well.

00:53:07.900 --> 00:53:10.390
And they were running
food to the poor

00:53:10.390 --> 00:53:13.130
and taking care of things
while he was not in town.

00:53:13.130 --> 00:53:16.090
And I had nuns in grade school.

00:53:16.090 --> 00:53:18.070
They can be wonderful,
and they can be fierce.

00:53:18.070 --> 00:53:21.100
And I'm sure I'm sure martial
law would have been preferable.

00:53:21.100 --> 00:53:21.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:21.900 --> 00:53:24.210
So thank you.

00:53:24.210 --> 00:53:28.620
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:28.620 --> 00:53:31.320
Thank you very much, everyone.

